WHY IT DECOUPLES (this is a proof, not a numeric).  Forward routing preserves the
ABSOLUTE degree d (the u-adic order at u=-1 is not rescaled by the shell), so the
bipartite graph is a DISJOINT UNION over d of forward-in-m chains.  The
neighbourhood-closed sets are therefore S = union_d {(m,d): m >= M_d}, and the cut
for such a union is the SUM of the per-d cuts -- implied by them.  Hence the
degree-resolved Hall condition is EXACTLY the family of per-(M,d) cuts tested above.

And the coarsest (degree-blind) cut is a VALID Hall cut for ANY routing rule in d,
since aggregating assumes the most generous possible d-mobility.  So opus's
violation below golden proves infeasibility UNCONDITIONALLY -- their stated gap
does not threaten the lower bound at all; degree resolution can only ADD
constraints, i.e. only RAISE the floor.  The test above shows it raises nothing.

